From ca30c8442e26e5119ddacb653081b79ada2b7d61 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 20:26:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][GBM] 3D: an HDR video drawn in each eye, under a GUI
 composited over the whole frame

Where the window system composites the GUI over the video (HDR10 and HLG passed through), the
video is not drawn in the GUI pass but after it, by CGUIWindowManager::RenderEx(), which
CApplication::Render() called once, after the passes of the eyes had set the view OFF again. In a
3D output the renderer then took the whole picture, both eyes of it, and drew it into the viewport
of the view OFF: the screen of one eye at the place of the left eye. The right eye got no video.
The black under the video (EndGuiComposite()) and the GUI composited from its FBO (CompositeGui())
went into that same viewport and scissor box: the GUI of both eyes squeezed into the left eye, and
the right eye kept whatever the buffer held before. Every HDR video in frame packing, side by side
or top and bottom was shown so (3D Blu-ray and MV-HEVC conversions in HDR10, Apple spatial video in
HLG, HLG 3D), as was a 3D HDR video played in 2D (both halves side by side over the screen) and an
anaglyph of one (no eye in its colours). An SDR video is drawn in the GUI pass of each eye and was
right.

The passes of the eyes are one rule now, KODI::RENDERING::STEREO_VIEWS::ForEachView(), the loop
that CApplication::Render() had for the GUI pass: the video after the composited GUI is drawn with
it too, in each eye's view, so that the renderer takes its half or view of the picture and the
place of its eye as it does in the GUI pass. Not where the video goes into an output of its own,
native Dolby Vision (CWinSystemBase::VideoLayerInEachEye()), which no 3D output has and which
draws once a frame. The clear and the composite are drawn over the whole buffer (CWholeBuffer,
which sets the viewport and the scissor box to it and puts them back), the eyes and the lines
between them. Without 3D both are the whole buffer already and nothing is set: a 2D video, HDR or
SDR, and an SDR video in 3D, are drawn exactly as before. kodi.log says it once a composite.
